I made the edit — the change is purely at the transport layer (setting `TCP_NODELAY` on each accepted socket via a custom `axum::serve::Listener` impl), so it doesn't touch authorization logic, response bodies, headers, or status codes.

Rationale: the benchmark's own numbers point at the network layer, not the handler. At 16 concurrent clients with p50 ≈ 64ms, throughput ≈ concurrency/latency ≈ 250 rps — exactly what's observed. But the actual handler work (a lock read, a struct clone, a few thousand hash iterations over ~40 bytes) is microseconds, not milliseconds — nowhere near enough to explain 64ms. That gap, combined with the classic ~40ms magnitude, is the signature of Nagle's algorithm (server-side) interacting with delayed ACKs (client-side) on small request/response pairs over a kept-alive connection — a well-known default-off `TCP_NODELAY` bug. The previous round's ~2.7% gain is consistent with someone having optimized the handler's CPU work (which isn't the bottleneck) instead of the socket option.

